{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfef8iC8Apu5kfb4V3MMK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote all three files for candidate 1, and the self-check prints `OK`. But the model looks likely to be pruned, and it may not finish fitting within admission's 30-minute limit.\n\n**Hypothesis (`exemplar_designed_pattern_similarity`):** people remember examples of \"designed\" coin sequences: streaks (HHHH), strict alternation (HTHT), and short repeating units (HHT, HHTT, HHHT, HTTT, with their shifts and mirror images). A sequence looks less random the more it resembles those stored patterns. Resemblance drops off exponentially with how many flips you would have to change to turn the sequence into a stored pattern, and it is added up over all the stored examples. Remembered random examples are too vague to favour any particular sequence, so people pick the sequence that resembles the designed examples less. People differ in how strongly they have stored the alternation examples.\n\n**Model (`candidate.py`):** `compute_features` returns, for each sequence, how many stored examples sit at each number of changed flips (0–8), counted separately for alternation and for the other patterns. The model has four parts:\n- one fitted rate for how fast resemblance drops off;\n- one decision sensitivity;\n- a per-person weight on the alternation examples, drawn from a population distribution with 400 slots, so the next design can predict new participants;\n- `p_left`, which compares how much the two sequences resemble the stored patterns.\n\nThis model goes at the critique's long-streak problem directly: a sequence with a long run is close to the streak examples, so it is penalised.\n\n**Self-check (100 draws, 1 chain):**\n- **Fit:** ELPD-LOO came out at about −1715. The best model's full fit is at −1070, so unless the full fit gives a very different number, this one would be pruned as having lost.\n- **Reliability